1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,710
Hello, world. Some news is big. Some news is weird.

2
00:00:04,710 --> 00:00:07,021
Tonight, most of it is both.

3
00:00:07,021 --> 00:00:10,900
I'm Master K, and this is the Four Eyes Report.

4
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:19,831
Coming up: a shark that won't leave, a typhoon that never came, Psy, and a refrigerator you can date.

5
00:00:19,831 --> 00:00:20,850
Let's begin.

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:26,240
Our top story: a shark. In a canal. In the middle of a city.

7
00:00:26,590 --> 00:00:33,599
On September 18th, a three-and-a-half-meter shark swam into a narrow canal at Busan's North Port.

8
00:00:33,599 --> 00:00:35,550
And then it just... stayed.

9
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,861
Officials tried to guide it back to the sea.

10
00:00:38,861 --> 00:00:40,140
The shark declined.

11
00:00:40,490 --> 00:00:44,010
It declined? Core, a shark cannot decline things.

12
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:49,240
It's K. And technically, it can. It simply did not leave.

13
00:00:49,590 --> 00:00:51,350
What kind of shark is it, anyway?

14
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:55,267
Probably a dusky shark. Or a copper shark.

15
00:00:55,267 --> 00:00:57,220
It declined to comment.

16
00:00:57,570 --> 00:00:59,853
So Korea did what Korea does.

17
00:00:59,853 --> 00:01:02,922
It gave the shark a nickname: Bukang-i.

18
00:01:02,922 --> 00:01:07,330
That's 'North Port', plus a cute little 'ee' on the end.

19
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:11,760
Wait. wait. It has a nickname? Does it have a fandom?

20
00:01:12,110 --> 00:01:13,916
Let's look at the numbers.

21
00:01:13,916 --> 00:01:16,485
Nearly six hundred thousand visitors.

22
00:01:16,485 --> 00:01:22,110
Over the Chuseok holiday, one hundred forty thousand people came in a single day.

23
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:27,082
On a normal holiday, this park gets about two thousand.

24
00:01:27,082 --> 00:01:30,780
That is a fiftyfold increase. For one shark.

25
00:01:31,130 --> 00:01:35,520
Six hundred thousand people in ten days? That's not a shark.

26
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,130
That's a stadium tour.

27
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:43,676
On Tuesday, five boats with water cannons spent hours trying to guide it back to sea.

28
00:01:43,676 --> 00:01:47,320
Bukang-i swam behind the boats. Every single time.

29
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:49,670
It declined. again.

30
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:53,400
So Busan made it an honorary ambassador.

31
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:57,540
Next plan: a custom-made net, as early as Friday.

32
00:01:57,890 --> 00:02:08,436
K's Take: Only in Korea does a lost shark refuse a rescue, draw six hundred thousand fans, and land a government job.

33
00:02:08,436 --> 00:02:10,690
Bukang-i, take your time.

34
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:14,159
Okay. A shark that won't leave.

35
00:02:14,159 --> 00:02:16,700
What's next, weather that won't arrive?

36
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:18,330
...Did you read my script?

37
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:19,934
Next: the weather.

38
00:02:19,934 --> 00:02:22,999
Or rather, the weather that never showed up.

39
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:27,999
This year, not a single typhoon has made landfall in Korea.

40
00:02:27,999 --> 00:02:28,630
Not one.

41
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:33,140
That's amazing! A free summer! Why do you look worried?

42
00:02:33,490 --> 00:02:36,690
I always look like this. But yes, I'm worried.

43
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:46,000
For three summers now, two giant high-pressure systems, one from the Pacific and one from Tibet, have parked around Korea like bouncers outside a club.

44
00:02:46,350 --> 00:02:53,790
In August, Typhoon Dolphin crossed the Pacific, saw the line at the door, and headed toward Shanghai instead.

45
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:56,700
Rejected at the door. Brutal.

46
00:02:57,050 --> 00:03:00,521
Here's the catch. Typhoons stir up the ocean.

47
00:03:00,521 --> 00:03:05,999
They mix cold, oxygen-rich water from below with the warm water on top.

48
00:03:05,999 --> 00:03:10,010
Scientists call them the ocean's ventilation system.

49
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:14,920
So the ocean spent all summer in a stuffy room with the windows shut.

50
00:03:15,270 --> 00:03:17,644
Exactly. And fish farms are feeling it.

51
00:03:17,644 --> 00:03:23,121
Along the southern coast, about seven point seven million farmed fish have died this year.

52
00:03:23,121 --> 00:03:27,990
Rainfall is down about fifteen percent, and parts of the country are in drought.

53
00:03:28,340 --> 00:03:30,500
Okay. That's not a free summer at all.

54
00:03:30,850 --> 00:03:35,810
And experts warn: fewer typhoons can mean stronger ones later.

55
00:03:35,810 --> 00:03:37,330
Warm water is fuel.

56
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:42,236
K's Take: A quiet summer isn't always a good summer.

57
00:03:42,236 --> 00:03:45,040
Sometimes the calm is the story.

58
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:49,050
Can we please talk about something happy now?

59
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:50,920
I have Psy.

60
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:51,910
Perfect.

61
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:55,821
Our next story comes from a neighborhood you already know.

62
00:03:55,821 --> 00:03:57,540
Even if you think you don't.

63
00:03:57,890 --> 00:04:00,930
Gangnam. Yes. That Gangnam.

64
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:03,173
Wait. Is this about the horse dance?

65
00:04:03,173 --> 00:04:05,840
Please tell me this is about the horse dance.

66
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:07,728
It is about a festival.

67
00:04:07,728 --> 00:04:18,430
This weekend is the fifteenth Gangnam Festival: a one-kilometer street parade, giant character balloons, and a traditional Korean marching band leading the way.

68
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:26,546
And on Sunday, a K-pop concert closed out by the man who taught the entire planet to say 'Gangnam'.

69
00:04:26,546 --> 00:04:26,860
Psy.

70
00:04:27,210 --> 00:04:28,409
Psy. In Gangnam.

71
00:04:28,409 --> 00:04:31,930
That is the biggest home game in music history!

72
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:35,152
Thirteen thousand free concert tickets.

73
00:04:35,152 --> 00:04:36,920
Gone almost immediately.

74
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:39,750
Free?! I would have fought someone for those.

75
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:44,756
This is a serious news program. We do not fight for tickets.

76
00:04:44,756 --> 00:04:47,860
We refresh the page calmly, like adults.

77
00:04:48,210 --> 00:04:55,293
Organizers even studied New York's Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade and Japan's Nebuta Festival.

78
00:04:55,293 --> 00:04:59,890
The goal: when people think 'K-festival', they think Gangnam.

79
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:02,000
Admit it. You're going.

80
00:05:02,350 --> 00:05:05,070
I have... prior commitments.

81
00:05:05,420 --> 00:05:14,025
K's Take: Fourteen years after one song put Gangnam on the world map, Gangnam is still trying to live up to it.

82
00:05:14,025 --> 00:05:15,420
Honestly? Respect.

83
00:05:16,870 --> 00:05:18,790
Speaking of prior commitments...

84
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:21,937
Finally tonight, a money story.

85
00:05:21,937 --> 00:05:26,900
In Korea, you can subscribe to... your air conditioner.

86
00:05:27,250 --> 00:05:29,570
Like Netflix? For an air conditioner?

87
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:30,484
Sort of.

88
00:05:30,484 --> 00:05:37,107
Samsung says its appliance subscription business is now approaching one trillion won in sales.

89
00:05:37,107 --> 00:05:40,560
That's about seven hundred forty million dollars.

90
00:05:40,910 --> 00:05:47,166
You pay monthly, and for the whole contract, engineers take care of maintenance, repairs and check-ups.

91
00:05:47,166 --> 00:05:50,750
It's less like streaming, and more like a very long rental.

92
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:54,335
So I don't own the fridge. The fridge and I are...

93
00:05:54,335 --> 00:05:55,500
in a relationship.

94
00:05:55,850 --> 00:05:59,530
A six-year relationship. With scheduled check-ins.

95
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:01,661
Let's look at the numbers.

96
00:06:01,661 --> 00:06:07,209
Ninety-four point eight percent of Koreans used a subscription service this year.

97
00:06:07,209 --> 00:06:09,880
The average person has five point five.

98
00:06:10,230 --> 00:06:13,350
Five point five? Who has half a subscription?

99
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:15,956
Technically, it's an average.

100
00:06:15,956 --> 00:06:19,380
Nobody has half a subscription. ...Probably.

101
00:06:19,730 --> 00:06:24,718
Now Samsung wants hotels, hospitals and public offices to subscribe too.

102
00:06:24,718 --> 00:06:32,130
There's even a plan for newlyweds and people who move a lot, with cleaning visits at night and on holidays.

103
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:35,366
Night cleaning visits? That's not a subscription.

104
00:06:35,366 --> 00:06:36,720
That's a love language.

105
00:06:37,070 --> 00:06:42,250
K's Take: In Korea, even your washing machine comes with a care plan.

106
00:06:42,250 --> 00:06:48,030
We're still deciding whether that's the future, or just very, very organized.

107
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:50,768
You can also subscribe to this channel.

108
00:06:50,768 --> 00:06:52,140
No six-year contract.

109
00:06:52,490 --> 00:06:55,055
That's all for tonight. Stay curious.

110
00:06:55,055 --> 00:06:56,650
Keep your lenses clean.

111
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:58,760
So what are you doing this weekend?

112
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:01,590
Refreshing pages. Calmly.

